cases.notes:
Load: 1.07, 1.10 and 1.19 s; peak footprint about 0.8 GB. assets.describe gives the aabb in raw file units (250000..250630, 0..200).

As placed with scale 0.001 and the offset subtracted (captures/machined.png), the plate stands up as a wall and the tower points at the camera: the Z-up model is lying on its side. Adding rotation {pitch:-90} with the matching position fixes it (machined_upright.png), so the workaround works, but the agent has to know to apply it.

Shading is wrong because the file has no vn, so normals are smoothed per position across 90-degree edges:
- the square tower renders as a rounded pillar;
- every drilled hole reads as a raised stud with a gradient ring;
- the plate's sides get vertical stripes;
- gear flanks and the gear cap are smeared.
Compare with Blender's flat import in captures/compare_machined_near.png.

At a 250 m offset there was no visible jitter or z-fighting once the part was moved back to the origin; float32 has about 0.016 mm steps at that distance. Blender's import takes 0.38 s; the C reader 0.09 s.

Negative indices for v, vt and vn are read correctly: the floor and all prisms are in the right places with the right normals. Quads are correct.

Concave n-gons are fanned from their first corner (import.cpp:194), so the result depends on where the exporter starts the polygon. 4 of the 8 test polygons come out wrong:
- the L that starts at its tip has its notch filled in;
- the star that starts at an outer tip is malformed;
- the comb's gaps are filled;
- the 18-corner C becomes a solid disc.
The L starting at a corner, the L starting at the reflex corner and the star starting at an inner vertex come out right only by luck. Blender renders all 8 correctly (blender_ngons.png).
